<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             x:Class="CcpClient.Desktop.Views.Pages.StudioPage">
  <!--
    The Studio rack (wpf-surface-reachability.md §8.3 @ 7527243e7, observed live at v6.8.1): grouped rows
    down the left, one panel on the right, left-click a row to open its panel. WPF has four
    groups and fifteen rows; the port now has ALL FOUR GROUPS and TEN rows, and all ten are
    real effects. Five of WPF's rows are still unported — Visuals, Bubble Pop, Bouncing Text,
    Haptics and Scheduler (StudioTabView.xaml.cs:496, :499, :505, :519, :535) — and none of them
    has a row here, which is WPF's own rule for a row that cannot be wired honestly (:494-496).
    Bubble Count lands here as the first row that consumes capabilities it did not shape.

    Rack ORDER is WPF's own (StudioTabView.xaml.cs:483-541): within EFFECTS, flash, video,
    subliminal, spiral, pinkfilter. Mandatory Video landed in its own upstream position,
    SECOND, so the rows no longer close up around it. Where a row IS still unported the ported
    ones close up around it without reordering the ones that are here, which is why the order of
    what is present is upstream's rather than the order the modules happened to land in.

    Flash Images landed with the two things §9 D5/D6 recorded as missing "because the port
    has nothing to wire yet" — a LIVE STATE DOT and a RIGHT-CLICK QUICK-TOGGLE. Then came the
    rows for Subliminals (which landed with no way to switch it on at all — D72) and for
    Pink Filter, the first continuous module. All three carry the full grammar, and they carry it
    because there is a real effect behind each one whose state can be reported and whose dial can
    be flipped.

    The last one wired was Spiral Overlay — the first module whose picture has to keep
    CHANGING while it is on — and with it D5/D6 CLOSE for the whole rack: every row here now
    carries the full grammar because every row here now has a real effect behind it. WPF's rule
    for a row it cannot wire honestly ("A dot that cannot be wired honestly is omitted",
    StudioTabView.xaml.cs:494-496) still governs the rows this port has not reached; there are
    simply none of them on this page any more.
  -->
  <Grid ColumnDefinitions="248,*" Margin="20">
    <!-- THE RACK SCROLLS, and it is a defect fix rather than a flourish. At ten rows the
         column is taller than the window's 760 px, so the LAST row was clipped out of the visible
         region and could not be clicked at all — measured, not supposed: a headless click at the
         row's own centre did not select it. WPF's own rack lives in a scrollable tab body for the
         same reason at fifteen rows. Auto, so nothing changes for a rack that fits. -->
    <ScrollViewer Grid.Column="0"
                  x:Name="RackScroll"
                  HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled"
                  VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto">
    <StackPanel Spacing="4">
      <TextBlock Classes="page-title" Text="Studio" />
      <TextBlock Classes="page-blurb" Text="Every effect, one rack. Pick a module on the left." />
      <!-- The grammar, said out loud. WPF surfaces it in a rail chip tooltip (§2,
           Views/Tabs/SettingsTabView.xaml:453 — "left-click opens it, right-click turns it
           on") and again in the Studio onboarding card (§8.3). A second gesture nobody is told
           about is a second gesture nobody uses. -->
      <TextBlock Classes="page-blurb"
                 AutomationProperties.AutomationId="RackGrammarHint"
                 Text="Left-click a row to open it. Right-click a row to flip that effect on or off." />

      <Border Classes="rack" Margin="0,12,0,0">
        <StackPanel Spacing="2">
          <TextBlock Classes="rack-group" Text="EFFECTS" />

          <!-- The rack row is a RadioButton, as WPF's is (StudioTabView.xaml.cs:645-651) —
               single-selection, and its keyboard activation is the control's own, not a
               bespoke key handler. The dot lives on the ROW, right-aligned, as WPF's does
               (BuildRestingStrip: "art (or emoji) chip | caption | state dot"); the gesture
               belongs to the whole row and never to the 8px dot (:658-659). -->
          <RadioButton x:Name="RowFlashImages"
                       Classes="rack-row"
                       GroupName="rack"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="RowFlashImages"
                       AutomationProperties.Name="Flash Images rack row">
            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" MinWidth="200">
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="Flash Images" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              <Ellipse Grid.Column="1"
                       x:Name="FlashRowDot"
                       Classes="dot"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="FlashRowDot" />
            </Grid>
          </RadioButton>

          <!-- WPF's EFFECTS group is Flash Images, Mandatory Video, Subliminals, Spiral
               Overlay, Magenta Filter, Visuals (StudioTabView.xaml.cs:483-497), so this row sits
               SECOND. Its label says "(silent half)" and that string is load-bearing rather than
               cosmetic: the dot on this row goes Live while the picture moves, and that is only
               honest because the row names its own scope where the user reads it. -->
          <RadioButton x:Name="RowMandatoryVideo"
                       Classes="rack-row"
                       GroupName="rack"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="RowMandatoryVideo"
                       AutomationProperties.Name="Mandatory Video rack row">
            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" MinWidth="200">
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="Mandatory Video (silent half)" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              <Ellipse Grid.Column="1"
                       x:Name="MandatoryVideoRowDot"
                       Classes="dot"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="MandatoryVideoRowDot" />
            </Grid>
          </RadioButton>

          <RadioButton x:Name="RowSubliminals"
                       Classes="rack-row"
                       GroupName="rack"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="RowSubliminals"
                       AutomationProperties.Name="Subliminals rack row">
            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" MinWidth="200">
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="Subliminals" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              <Ellipse Grid.Column="1"
                       x:Name="SubliminalRowDot"
                       Classes="dot"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SubliminalRowDot" />
            </Grid>
          </RadioButton>

          <RadioButton x:Name="RowSpiralOverlay"
                       Classes="rack-row"
                       GroupName="rack"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="RowSpiralOverlay"
                       AutomationProperties.Name="Spiral Overlay rack row">
            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" MinWidth="200">
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="Spiral Overlay" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              <Ellipse Grid.Column="1"
                       x:Name="SpiralRowDot"
                       Classes="dot"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SpiralRowDot" />
            </Grid>
          </RadioButton>

          <!-- WPF's EFFECTS group carries Bouncing Text after Spiral Overlay; the row was
               absent from this port for nine waves because its window is transparency-backed glyphs
               (D83) and this port's overlay refused per-pixel alpha by design. The title names the
               ported half, on the precedent the earlier rows set. -->
          <RadioButton x:Name="RowBouncingText"
                       Classes="rack-row"
                       GroupName="rack"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="RowBouncingText"
                       AutomationProperties.Name="Bouncing Text rack row">
            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" MinWidth="200">
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="Bouncing Text (motion half)" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              <Ellipse Grid.Column="1"
                       x:Name="BouncingTextRowDot"
                       Classes="dot"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BouncingTextRowDot" />
            </Grid>
          </RadioButton>

          <RadioButton x:Name="RowPinkFilter"
                       Classes="rack-row"
                       GroupName="rack"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="RowPinkFilter"
                       AutomationProperties.Name="Pink Filter rack row">
            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" MinWidth="200">
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="Pink Filter" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              <Ellipse Grid.Column="1"
                       x:Name="PinkFilterRowDot"
                       Classes="dot"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="PinkFilterRowDot" />
            </Grid>
          </RadioButton>

          <!-- VISUALS, the EFFECTS group's sixth and last row, in WPF's own position
               (StudioTabView.xaml.cs:484-496: flash, video, subliminal, spiral, pinkfilter,
               visuals).

               IT HAS NO DOT, AND THE ABSENCE IS THE PORTED DECISION. Upstream's rack entry passes
               null where every other row passes a dot predicate (:496), because Visuals has no
               master toggle to read: it is not a module, it is the Flash Images module's own draw
               dials on their own page, and there is no VisualsService anywhere in the shipping
               tree. Upstream says so at :494-495 — "A dot that cannot be wired honestly is
               omitted". A dot here could only ever be the Flash Images row's dot, repeated two
               inches to its left. This is the only row in the rack whose Grid has one child. -->
          <RadioButton x:Name="RowVisuals"
                       Classes="rack-row"
                       GroupName="rack"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="RowVisuals"
                       AutomationProperties.Name="Visuals rack row">
            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" MinWidth="200">
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="Visuals" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            </Grid>
          </RadioButton>

          <!-- GAMES & CARDS, and it is the group the IMMERSION comment said the port did
               not have. WPF's group order is EFFECTS, GAMES & CARDS, IMMERSION, TIMING
               (StudioTabView.xaml.cs:483/498/508/530), so it opens here, between the two that
               already exist, and nothing is reordered. The header is upstream's own string
               (st4_studio_group_games, en.json).

               ITS ONE ROW IS THE ONLY MODULE ON THIS PAGE THAT ASKS THE USER ANYTHING. Every
               other row draws, tints, paces, ramps or plays over whatever the user is doing;
               this one takes the keyboard away from it and waits. Its dot therefore means
               something no earlier dot has meant — see LockCardEffect. -->
          <TextBlock Classes="rack-group" Text="GAMES &amp; CARDS" />

          <!-- Bubble Pop goes FIRST in this group because that is where the rack puts it:
               Bubble Pop, Bubble Count, Lock Card, Bouncing Text (StudioTabView.xaml.cs:499-505).
               The Bubble Count comment below already named this position as Bubble Pop's and said the
               row was unported; it is ported now and takes the place that was held for it.

               THIS ROW IS THE FIRST THE USER ACTS ON. Every module before it did something TO
               them. Its dot therefore means something no earlier dot has meant: not that a
               picture is on the screen or that a question is in front of them, but that the
               OPERATING SYSTEM will route a click at one of these windows to this app — a claim
               on attention the OS grants and can revoke, which is the Lock Card row's DEMAND with the hit
               test in place of the foreground. See BubblePopEffect. -->
          <RadioButton x:Name="RowBubblePop"
                       Classes="rack-row"
                       GroupName="rack"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="RowBubblePop"
                       AutomationProperties.Name="Bubble Pop rack row">
            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" MinWidth="200">
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="Bubble Pop" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              <Ellipse Grid.Column="1"
                       x:Name="BubblePopRowDot"
                       Classes="dot"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BubblePopRowDot" />
            </Grid>
          </RadioButton>

          <!-- Bubble Count goes ABOVE Lock Card because the RACK's order is the one the port
               takes when the two upstream orders disagree, which they do here: the rack is Bubble
               Pop, Bubble Count, Lock Card, Bouncing Text (StudioTabView.xaml.cs:499-505) while
               StartEngine starts the lock card first and the bubble count second (:206-215). D90
               settled that question for Spiral/Pink Filter and the same answer applies: the rack is
               the order the user has learned. Bubble Pop is the row directly above.

               THIS ROW IS THE FIRST THAT USES TWO CAPABILITIES. It plays a clip on the same video
               surface Mandatory Video plays on and asks its question through the same input
               presence the Lock Card asks through, and its dot therefore means BOTH of the two
               things those rows' dots mean, one at a time — see BubbleCountEffect. -->
          <RadioButton x:Name="RowBubbleCount"
                       Classes="rack-row"
                       GroupName="rack"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="RowBubbleCount"
                       AutomationProperties.Name="Bubble Count rack row">
            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" MinWidth="200">
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="Bubble Count" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              <Ellipse Grid.Column="1"
                       x:Name="BubbleCountRowDot"
                       Classes="dot"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BubbleCountRowDot" />
            </Grid>
          </RadioButton>

          <RadioButton x:Name="RowLockCard"
                       Classes="rack-row"
                       GroupName="rack"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="RowLockCard"
                       AutomationProperties.Name="Lock Card rack row">
            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" MinWidth="200">
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="Lock Card" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              <Ellipse Grid.Column="1"
                       x:Name="LockCardRowDot"
                       Classes="dot"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="LockCardRowDot" />
            </Grid>
          </RadioButton>

          <!-- POP QUIZ CLOSES GAMES & CARDS, and its position is the PORT's rather than a copy of
               anything: upstream has no Studio rack row for this module at all — its two dials live
               on the Graded Intake door (Views/Tabs/GradedIntakeTabView.xaml:255-292) — so there is
               no upstream rack order to take. It goes with the other three cards because that is
               what it is, and it goes last in the group because it is the newest and moving the
               three above it would reorder rows a user has already learned.

               ITS DOT IS THE LOCK CARD'S, exactly: a firing on the clock, a desktop this process can
               put a window on, and — while a card of ITS OWN is up — the operating system's own
               confirmation that the card holds the input. See PopQuizEffect.WorkIsRunning, and note
               the `of MINE` qualifier there: the presence is shared with two rows above, so a bare
               IsPrompting would darken this row for somebody else's card. -->
          <RadioButton x:Name="RowPopQuiz"
                       Classes="rack-row"
                       GroupName="rack"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="RowPopQuiz"
                       AutomationProperties.Name="Pop Quiz rack row">
            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" MinWidth="200">
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="Pop Quiz" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              <Ellipse Grid.Column="1"
                       x:Name="PopQuizRowDot"
                       Classes="dot"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="PopQuizRowDot" />
            </Grid>
          </RadioButton>

          <!-- THE RACK'S THIRD GROUP, and it sits HERE rather than at the end
               because WPF's group order is EFFECTS, GAMES & CARDS, IMMERSION, TIMING
               (StudioTabView.xaml.cs:483/498/508/530). GAMES & CARDS opened above with
               its one ported row; the rest of that group still needs video or a moving
               clickable window. The header is upstream's own string
               (st4_studio_group_immersion = "IMMERSION", en.json:4818).

               BOTH ROWS ARE AUDIO, and they are the first rows on this page whose output nobody
               in this process can see. Their dots therefore mean something no earlier dot has
               meant: a firing on the clock AND an audio render session the OPERATING SYSTEM
               confirms belongs to this process. See AudioCueEffect. -->
          <TextBlock Classes="rack-group" Text="IMMERSION" />

          <RadioButton x:Name="RowMindWipe"
                       Classes="rack-row"
                       GroupName="rack"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="RowMindWipe"
                       AutomationProperties.Name="Mind Wipe rack row">
            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" MinWidth="200">
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="Mind Wipe" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              <Ellipse Grid.Column="1"
                       x:Name="MindWipeRowDot"
                       Classes="dot"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="MindWipeRowDot" />
            </Grid>
          </RadioButton>

          <!-- THE LABEL IS LOAD-BEARING. Upstream's row is "Brain Drain" and drives two
               mechanisms off one flag; this port has one of them. The suffix is what makes the
               dot beside it honest, because the dot is scoped to what the row says it is —
               rename this to plain "Brain Drain" and a truthful Live becomes a false one. -->
          <RadioButton x:Name="RowBrainDrain"
                       Classes="rack-row"
                       GroupName="rack"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="RowBrainDrain"
                       AutomationProperties.Name="Brain Drain audio half rack row">
            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" MinWidth="200">
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="Brain Drain (audio half)" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap" />
              <Ellipse Grid.Column="1"
                       x:Name="BrainDrainRowDot"
                       Classes="dot"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BrainDrainRowDot" />
            </Grid>
          </RadioButton>

          <!-- THE LAST ROW IN IMMERSION, and upstream's own position: Haptics is added
               immediately after Brain Drain and before the TIMING group header
               (StudioTabView.xaml.cs:513/519/530). It is upstream's one PAID rack module
               (`tier: 1`, :526-527 — "the rack's one paid module"), and it is the first row on
               this page behind a premium gate.

               ITS DOT IS EARNED, AND IT HAS ONLY TWO REACHABLE VALUES. Upstream passes a dot
               predicate here (() => App.Settings?.Current?.Haptics?.Enabled, :520), so the row
               HAS a dot, unlike Visuals' null at :496. Off = the switch is off, or nothing in
               this build can reach a device. Armed = the switch is on and a device really is
               reachable. LIVE IS UNREACHABLE, deliberately: it would have to mean something is
               being sent, and nothing is — the thirteen ported effect modules are silent to this
               sink where upstream drives it from THIRTEEN sites in three of them. See
               Haptics/HapticParticipant.Dot, and IHapticSink for the enumeration.

               THE ROW TAKES THE RIGHT-CLICK, and it is the one gesture on this page that can be
               REFUSED. Upstream's toggle flips the panel's own master box so the real handler
               runs "including the premium gate that reverts the box for a free account"
               (:521-525), and its rack re-reads the dot one beat later because "a refusal can
               undo the write (the haptics premium gate flips IsChecked back)" (:1121-1124). -->
          <RadioButton x:Name="RowHaptics"
                       Classes="rack-row"
                       GroupName="rack"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="RowHaptics"
                       AutomationProperties.Name="Haptics rack row">
            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" MinWidth="200">
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="Haptics" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              <Ellipse Grid.Column="1"
                       x:Name="HapticsRowDot"
                       Classes="dot"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="HapticsRowDot" />
            </Grid>
          </RadioButton>

          <!-- AUDIO, AND IT IS THE FIRST ROW ON THIS RACK THAT IS NOT A MODULE AT ALL.
               Upstream keeps these four controls in its SETTINGS door's audio card
               (Views/Controls/AppSettings/AudioSettingsSection.xaml:82, :103, :181, :195), not in
               its rack — this port has no settings door, and the app-wide device row it does have
               is Haptics, immediately above. So it goes beside it, at the foot of IMMERSION, where
               the two rows a session never borrows sit together and directly under the two modules
               (Mind Wipe, Brain Drain) whose whole output these dials govern.

               NO DOT, and it is the Visuals row's precedent rather than an omission
               (StudioPage.axaml:167-186; upstream passes null where every other row passes a dot
               predicate, StudioTabView.xaml.cs:494-496). A dot on this rack means Off / Armed /
               Live for a MODULE: switched off, waiting for the session, running. This row has no
               enable to switch, nothing to arm and no schedule to be live on. What the operating
               system last answered about a device is a fact with three of its own values and it
               belongs in the panel's notice, in words, where it can say "nothing has been asked
               yet" — which no dot can.

               NO RIGHT-CLICK either, for the same reason and the same upstream case: the rack's
               quick-toggle flips a module's enable, and rows with none fall through unhandled
               (StudioTabView.xaml.cs:659).

               NOT ONE CONTROL IN THE PANEL BELOW IS session-owned. Read the SessionOwnedMarker
               remarks in StudioPage.axaml.cs before adding the class here: the rule is that a dial
               locks iff its document is one of the eleven a run borrows AND upstream marks it
               owned, and audio.json fails BOTH halves. ScriptedSessionDials' constructor does not
               take it (Session/ScriptedSessionDials.cs:51-61), so nothing snapshots it and nothing
               writes it back; and volumes are named in upstream's own never-lock list as COMFORT
               (Features/SessionLock.cs:21-38). A session does not get to move the user's master
               volume or re-route their headphones. -->
          <RadioButton x:Name="RowAudio"
                       Classes="rack-row"
                       GroupName="rack"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="RowAudio"
                       AutomationProperties.Name="Audio rack row">
            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*" MinWidth="200">
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="Audio" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            </Grid>
          </RadioButton>

          <!-- THE RACK'S SECOND GROUP. WPF has four (EFFECTS, GAMES & CARDS,
               IMMERSION, TIMING — §8.3, built at StudioTabView.xaml.cs:483/498/508/530); the
               port now has two, because it now has a module in a second one. The group header
               is upstream's own string (st4_studio_group_timing = "TIMING", en.json:4819) and
               the group exists because a module needs it, not to fill the rack out.

               THE SCHEDULER ROW COMPLETES THIS GROUP, and the sentence that used to end this comment —
               "the other TIMING row, Scheduler, starts the engine from OUTSIDE a session and
               is therefore not a session module at all" — is still TRUE and is no longer a
               reason it has no row. It is not on SessionEngine.Effects and never will be; it
               is owned at APP lifetime (Scheduling/SchedulerParticipant.cs) and it drives the
               engine rather than living in it. Upstream's rack puts it FIRST in TIMING, before
               Intensity Ramp (StudioTabView.xaml.cs:535-539), which is where it goes. -->
          <TextBlock Classes="rack-group" Text="TIMING" />

          <!-- THE ONLY ROW ON THIS PAGE THAT CAN START A SESSION. Every other row acts inside
               one somebody started; this one begins one on a clock, with nobody at the
               keyboard. Its dot therefore means something no earlier dot has meant, and it is
               NOT a capability claim — this module needs none of the port's six. Live means:
               the enable is on, a tick is REALLY on the clock (the 60 s start-up grace has
               elapsed and the poll is armed), and the LOCAL clock is inside the user's window
               right now. Off covers both "switched off" and "cannot act yet", because to a
               user those are the same thing. See SessionScheduler.Dot.

               Upstream passes a dot predicate here — () => App.Settings?.Current?.SchedulerEnabled
               (StudioTabView.xaml.cs:536) — unlike Visuals' null at :496, so this row has one;
               it is earned rather than read off the checkbox. -->
          <RadioButton x:Name="RowScheduler"
                       Classes="rack-row"
                       GroupName="rack"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="RowScheduler"
                       AutomationProperties.Name="Scheduler rack row">
            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" MinWidth="200">
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="Scheduler" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              <Ellipse Grid.Column="1"
                       x:Name="SchedulerRowDot"
                       Classes="dot"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SchedulerRowDot" />
            </Grid>
          </RadioButton>

          <RadioButton x:Name="RowIntensityRamp"
                       Classes="rack-row"
                       GroupName="rack"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="RowIntensityRamp"
                       AutomationProperties.Name="Intensity Ramp rack row">
            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" MinWidth="200">
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="Intensity Ramp" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              <Ellipse Grid.Column="1"
                       x:Name="RampRowDot"
                       Classes="dot"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="RampRowDot" />
            </Grid>
          </RadioButton>

          <!-- A FIFTH GROUP, and it is not a fifth group of MODULES: it is upstream's other
               Studio-door TAB. WPF's rail maps the studio door to three tabs — studio, presets and
               haptics (MainWindow/MainWindow.TabNavigation.cs:592) — and the session rack lives on
               the PRESETS one (Views/Tabs/PresetsTabView.xaml:857, SessionRackPanel). The four
               groups above are all the `studio` tab; haptics already folded in as a row; this is
               the third tab folding in the same way. That is also why it sits LAST rather than
               anywhere more convenient: `presets` comes after `studio` in the door's own entry
               list, and a scripted session is not a module in the rack above it.

               NO DOT, and no right-click quick-toggle. Both are the same fact: this row has no
               enable to flip. Upstream's rack lets exactly such a row fall through unhandled
               (Views/Tabs/StudioTabView.xaml.cs:659, and :494-496 for the dotless row), and the
               live state a dot would summarise is on this row's panel in full — the phase, the
               percentage and the time left. -->
          <TextBlock Classes="rack-group" Text="SESSIONS" />

          <RadioButton x:Name="RowScriptedSession"
                       Classes="rack-row"
                       GroupName="rack"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="RowScriptedSession"
                       AutomationProperties.Name="Scripted Sessions rack row">
            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*" MinWidth="200">
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="Scripted Sessions" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            </Grid>
          </RadioButton>
        </StackPanel>
      </Border>
    </StackPanel>
    </ScrollViewer>

    <Border Grid.Column="1" Classes="module" Margin="20,0,0,0">
      <DockPanel>
      <!-- THE SESSION FEATURE LOCK'S "WHY" (MainWindow/MainWindow.SessionFeatureLock.cs:203-227).
           Upstream injects one banner per open panel (:305-313) because its dials live in fourteen
           separate Features/*FeatureControl.xaml files; this rack's dials are all children of the
           one detail host below, so ONE banner docked above it is on screen for whichever panel is
           open. It is never blank while the lock is up, for upstream's stated reason: "a greyed-out
           control with no explanation reads as a bug" (:104-106).

           It is a DOCKED strip rather than upstream's zero-height overlay ribbon (:205-212). That
           ribbon exists because WPF's dashboard is a fixed 1489x901 design canvas that silently
           clips anything pushed past it; this page is a live-layout DockPanel with no such canvas,
           so the honest shape is the one that reserves its own height instead of covering a dial. -->
      <Border DockPanel.Dock="Top"
              x:Name="SessionLockBanner"
              Classes="notice"
              IsVisible="False"
              Margin="0,0,0,10">
        <TextBlock x:Name="SessionLockReason"
                   AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SessionLockReason"
                   Foreground="#FFFF69B4"
                   FontSize="12"
                   FontWeight="SemiBold"
                   TextWrapping="Wrap"
                   Text="" />
      </Border>
      <Panel>
        <TextBlock x:Name="RackHint"
                   Classes="page-blurb"
                   VerticalAlignment="Center" HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                   Text="Pick a module on the left." />

        <!-- ================= Flash Images ================= -->
        <StackPanel x:Name="FlashModulePanel" IsVisible="False" Spacing="10">
          <TextBlock Classes="module-title" Text="Flash Images" />
          <TextBlock Classes="page-blurb" Text="Images flash on a schedule while the session runs." />

          <!-- THREE DIALS, and every one of them is read by the running effect. WPF's panel has
               a dozen more (clickable, hydra, centre exclusion, solid mode, glow —
               Features/FlashFeatureControl.xaml, CCP.Core/Models/AppSettings.cs:749-960) and every
               one of those describes how a flash is DRAWN. Those the port has no drawing for are
               ABSENT rather than greyed: a disabled dial swallows the gesture and tells the user
               nothing (§9 D7).

               THREE OF THEM ARRIVED. Size, opacity and duration are now real dials, and
               they are on the VISUALS row rather than here, because that is where upstream puts
               them (Features/VisualsFeatureControl.xaml). This panel is the SCHEDULE; that one is
               the DRAW. -->
          <CheckBox x:Name="FlashEnableToggle"
                    Classes="dial-enable session-owned"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="FlashEnableToggle"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Flash Images enabled"
                    Content="Enable Flash Images" />

          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="150,*,Auto" RowDefinitions="Auto,Auto" Margin="0,4,0,0">
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Classes="dial-label" Text="Flashes per hour" />
            <Slider Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1"
                    x:Name="FlashFrequencySlider"
                    Classes="session-owned"
                    Minimum="1" Maximum="180"
                    SmallChange="1" LargeChange="10"
                    IsSnapToTickEnabled="True" TickFrequency="1"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="FlashFrequencySlider"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Flashes per hour" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="2"
                       x:Name="FlashFrequencyValue"
                       Classes="dial-value"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="FlashFrequencyValue"
                       Text="" />

            <TextBlock Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="0" Classes="dial-label" Text="Images per flash" />
            <Slider Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="1"
                    x:Name="FlashImagesSlider"
                    Classes="session-owned"
                    Minimum="1" Maximum="20"
                    SmallChange="1" LargeChange="5"
                    IsSnapToTickEnabled="True" TickFrequency="1"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="FlashImagesSlider"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Images per flash" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="2"
                       x:Name="FlashImagesValue"
                       Classes="dial-value"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="FlashImagesValue"
                       Text="" />
          </Grid>

          <!-- What the effect ACTUALLY did. Counts, never file names: the media-logging rule
               the DTRH manifest already holds. This is the surface that makes "the effect runs"
               and "the effect stopped" observable to a user with no overlay on screen. -->
          <Border Classes="notice">
            <StackPanel Spacing="4">
              <TextBlock x:Name="FlashLiveState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="FlashLiveState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
              <TextBlock x:Name="FlashPoolState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="FlashPoolState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         Opacity="0.7"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
            </StackPanel>
          </Border>

          <!-- WHERE THE IMAGES GO, read from the surface rather than asserted about the platform.
               This line used to say the drawing half was not ported; on Windows that became
               false the day the overlay landed, and on Linux it is still exactly right. A hard-coded
               sentence cannot be both, and a platform check here would be a second opinion about a
               capability that already answers for itself - so the text is derived from the
               presenter's own last CapabilityState, verbatim, reason code and all. Before a flash has
               been attempted it names the MECHANISM and says nothing has been drawn yet - which is
               a claim about this session, not about the screen, and it is still there before the
               user presses anything. -->
          <Border Classes="notice">
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       x:Name="FlashSurfaceState"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="FlashSurfaceState"
                       Text="" />
          </Border>
        </StackPanel>

        <!-- ================= Visuals ================= -->
        <!-- THE ONLY PANEL IN THE RACK WITH NO ENABLE CHECKBOX, matching upstream, whose
             own markup says why: "no enable pill: Visuals is the one module with no master toggle
             at all" (Features/VisualsFeatureControl.xaml:12-14). It carries no live-state line
             either, because it has no dot to explain: what it reports instead is whose dials these
             are and when a change lands. -->
        <StackPanel x:Name="VisualsModulePanel" IsVisible="False" Spacing="10">
          <TextBlock Classes="module-title" Text="Visuals" />
          <TextBlock Classes="page-blurb"
                     x:Name="VisualsOwnershipState"
                     TextWrapping="Wrap"
                     AutomationProperties.AutomationId="VisualsOwnershipState"
                     Text="" />

          <!-- THREE DIALS, and TWO DELIBERATE ABSENCES. Upstream's page has five controls; its
               Fade slider writes AppSettings.FadeDuration, which NOTHING in the shipping product
               reads (the fade rate is the constant FADE_PER_SEC = 2.4,
               Services/Flash/FlashService.cs:2018), and its Link-to-audio switch makes a flash last
               as long as its sound, which this port's silent flash cannot do. Both are ABSENT
               rather than greyed (§9 D7), and the panel says so out loud below. -->
          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="150,*,Auto" RowDefinitions="Auto,Auto,Auto" Margin="0,4,0,0">
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Classes="dial-label" Text="Size" />
            <Slider Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1"
                    x:Name="VisualsScaleSlider"
                    Classes="session-owned"
                    Minimum="50" Maximum="250"
                    SmallChange="1" LargeChange="10"
                    IsSnapToTickEnabled="True" TickFrequency="1"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="VisualsScaleSlider"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Flash image size" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="2"
                       x:Name="VisualsScaleValue"
                       Classes="dial-value"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="VisualsScaleValue"
                       Text="" />

            <TextBlock Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="0" Classes="dial-label" Text="Opacity" />
            <Slider Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="1"
                    x:Name="VisualsOpacitySlider"
                    Classes="session-owned"
                    Minimum="10" Maximum="100"
                    SmallChange="1" LargeChange="10"
                    IsSnapToTickEnabled="True" TickFrequency="1"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="VisualsOpacitySlider"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Flash image opacity" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="2"
                       x:Name="VisualsOpacityValue"
                       Classes="dial-value"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="VisualsOpacityValue"
                       Text="" />

            <TextBlock Grid.Row="2" Grid.Column="0" Classes="dial-label" Text="On screen for" />
            <Slider Grid.Row="2" Grid.Column="1"
                    x:Name="VisualsDurationSlider"
                    Classes="session-owned"
                    Minimum="1" Maximum="30"
                    SmallChange="1" LargeChange="5"
                    IsSnapToTickEnabled="True" TickFrequency="1"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="VisualsDurationSlider"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Flash image duration in seconds" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="2" Grid.Column="2"
                       x:Name="VisualsDurationValue"
                       Classes="dial-value"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="VisualsDurationValue"
                       Text="" />
          </Grid>

          <Border Classes="notice">
            <StackPanel Spacing="4">
              <TextBlock x:Name="VisualsDialState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="VisualsDialState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
              <TextBlock x:Name="VisualsAbsenceState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="VisualsAbsenceState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         Opacity="0.7"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
            </StackPanel>
          </Border>

          <Border Classes="notice">
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       x:Name="VisualsSurfaceState"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="VisualsSurfaceState"
                       Text="" />
          </Border>
        </StackPanel>

        <!-- ================= Subliminals ================= -->
        <StackPanel x:Name="MandatoryVideoModulePanel" IsVisible="False" Spacing="10">
          <TextBlock Classes="module-title" Text="Mandatory Video (silent half)" />
          <TextBlock Classes="page-blurb" Text="Plays one of your own clips, now and then, while the session runs." />

          <!-- THE MISSING HALF, first thing on the panel and not buried at the bottom. Its text is
               the module's own constant (MandatoryVideoEffect.VideoPanelNoticeText), which is the
               same string the arm result carries, so the panel and the typed outcome cannot drift
               into two different accounts of one absence. -->
          <Border Classes="notice">
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       x:Name="MandatoryVideoSilentHalfState"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="MandatoryVideoSilentHalfState"
                       Text="" />
          </Border>

          <CheckBox x:Name="MandatoryVideoEnableToggle"
                    Classes="dial-enable session-owned"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="MandatoryVideoEnableToggle"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Mandatory Video enabled"
                    Content="Enable Mandatory Video (silent half)" />

          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="150,*,Auto" RowDefinitions="Auto,Auto" Margin="0,4,0,0">
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Classes="dial-label" Text="Clips per hour" />
            <Slider Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1"
                    x:Name="MandatoryVideoFrequencySlider"
                    Classes="session-owned"
                    Minimum="1" Maximum="20"
                    SmallChange="1" LargeChange="5"
                    IsSnapToTickEnabled="True" TickFrequency="1"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="MandatoryVideoFrequencySlider"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Mandatory Video clips per hour" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="2"
                       x:Name="MandatoryVideoFrequencyValue"
                       Classes="dial-value"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="MandatoryVideoFrequencyValue"
                       Text="" />

            <TextBlock Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="0" Classes="dial-label" Text="Max length (s)" />
            <Slider Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="1"
                    x:Name="MandatoryVideoMaxLengthSlider"
                    Classes="session-owned"
                    Minimum="0" Maximum="600"
                    SmallChange="5" LargeChange="30"
                    IsSnapToTickEnabled="True" TickFrequency="5"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="MandatoryVideoMaxLengthSlider"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Mandatory Video max length" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="2"
                       x:Name="MandatoryVideoMaxLengthValue"
                       Classes="dial-value"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="MandatoryVideoMaxLengthValue"
                       Text="" />
          </Grid>

          <Border Classes="notice">
            <StackPanel Spacing="4">
              <TextBlock x:Name="MandatoryVideoLiveState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="MandatoryVideoLiveState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
              <TextBlock x:Name="MandatoryVideoClipState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="MandatoryVideoClipState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         Opacity="0.7"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
            </StackPanel>
          </Border>

          <!-- The capability's own answer, verbatim: what the OPERATING SYSTEM said about the last
               picture this module put on a surface. Never a sentence about a platform. -->
          <Border Classes="notice">
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       x:Name="MandatoryVideoSurfaceState"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="MandatoryVideoSurfaceState"
                       Text="" />
          </Border>
        </StackPanel>

        <StackPanel x:Name="SubliminalModulePanel" IsVisible="False" Spacing="10">
          <TextBlock Classes="module-title" Text="Subliminals" />
          <TextBlock Classes="page-blurb" Text="A phrase flashes over your screen on a schedule while the session runs." />

          <!-- TWO dials, and both are read by the running effect. WPF's panel also carries a card
               duration, a card opacity, the whisper-audio pair and the phrase-pool editor
               (Features/SubliminalFeatureControl.xaml). The pool editor is a surface of its own and
               the audio half is not ported at all (D68); the two remaining sliders move numbers the
               port does honour, and they arrive with the panel that has room for them. A disabled
               dial swallows the gesture and tells the user nothing (§9 D7), so what is not here is
               absent rather than greyed. -->
          <CheckBox x:Name="SubliminalEnableToggle"
                    Classes="dial-enable session-owned"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SubliminalEnableToggle"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Subliminals enabled"
                    Content="Enable Subliminals" />

          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="150,*,Auto" RowDefinitions="Auto" Margin="0,4,0,0">
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Classes="dial-label" Text="Messages per minute" />
            <Slider Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1"
                    x:Name="SubliminalFrequencySlider"
                    Classes="session-owned"
                    Minimum="1" Maximum="30"
                    SmallChange="1" LargeChange="5"
                    IsSnapToTickEnabled="True" TickFrequency="1"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SubliminalFrequencySlider"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Messages per minute" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="2"
                       x:Name="SubliminalFrequencyValue"
                       Classes="dial-value"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SubliminalFrequencyValue"
                       Text="" />
          </Grid>

          <Border Classes="notice">
            <StackPanel Spacing="4">
              <TextBlock x:Name="SubliminalLiveState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SubliminalLiveState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
              <TextBlock x:Name="SubliminalPoolState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SubliminalPoolState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         Opacity="0.7"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
            </StackPanel>
          </Border>

          <Border Classes="notice">
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       x:Name="SubliminalSurfaceState"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SubliminalSurfaceState"
                       Text="" />
          </Border>
        </StackPanel>

        <!-- ================= Bouncing Text ================= -->
        <StackPanel x:Name="BouncingTextModulePanel" IsVisible="False" Spacing="10">
          <TextBlock Classes="module-title" Text="Bouncing Text (motion half)" />
          <TextBlock Classes="page-blurb" Text="DVD-style bouncing words across the screen" />

          <!-- The panel LEADS with what is missing, positionally and not only in text: the six
               per-frame transform effects are not ported and TWO of them ship ON upstream, so a
               user who never reads a record still learns the scope before enabling anything. Same
               shape and same reason as the video row's silent-half notice. -->
          <Border Classes="notice">
            <TextBlock x:Name="BouncingTextAbsentNotice"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BouncingTextAbsentNotice"
                       TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       Text="" />
          </Border>

          <CheckBox x:Name="BouncingTextEnableToggle"
                    Classes="dial-enable session-owned"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BouncingTextEnableToggle"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Bouncing Text enabled"
                    Content="Enable Bouncing Text" />

          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="150,*,Auto" RowDefinitions="Auto,Auto,Auto" Margin="0,4,0,0">
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Classes="dial-label" Text="Speed" />
            <Slider Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1"
                    x:Name="BouncingTextSpeedSlider"
                    Classes="session-owned"
                    Minimum="1" Maximum="10"
                    SmallChange="1" LargeChange="2"
                    IsSnapToTickEnabled="True" TickFrequency="1"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BouncingTextSpeedSlider"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Bouncing Text speed" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="2"
                       x:Name="BouncingTextSpeedValue"
                       Classes="dial-value"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BouncingTextSpeedValue"
                       Text="" />

            <TextBlock Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="0" Classes="dial-label" Text="Size" />
            <Slider Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="1"
                    x:Name="BouncingTextSizeSlider"
                    Classes="session-owned"
                    Minimum="50" Maximum="300"
                    SmallChange="5" LargeChange="25"
                    IsSnapToTickEnabled="True" TickFrequency="5"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BouncingTextSizeSlider"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Bouncing Text size" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="2"
                       x:Name="BouncingTextSizeValue"
                       Classes="dial-value"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BouncingTextSizeValue"
                       Text="" />

            <TextBlock Grid.Row="2" Grid.Column="0" Classes="dial-label" Text="Opacity" />
            <Slider Grid.Row="2" Grid.Column="1"
                    x:Name="BouncingTextOpacitySlider"
                    Minimum="0" Maximum="100"
                    SmallChange="1" LargeChange="10"
                    IsSnapToTickEnabled="True" TickFrequency="1"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BouncingTextOpacitySlider"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Bouncing Text opacity" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="2" Grid.Column="2"
                       x:Name="BouncingTextOpacityValue"
                       Classes="dial-value"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BouncingTextOpacityValue"
                       Text="" />
          </Grid>

          <Border Classes="notice">
            <StackPanel Spacing="4">
              <TextBlock x:Name="BouncingTextLiveState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BouncingTextLiveState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
              <TextBlock x:Name="BouncingTextPoolState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BouncingTextPoolState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         Opacity="0.7"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
            </StackPanel>
          </Border>

          <Border Classes="notice">
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       x:Name="BouncingTextSurfaceState"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BouncingTextSurfaceState"
                       Text="" />
          </Border>
        </StackPanel>

        <!-- ================= Spiral Overlay ================= -->
        <StackPanel x:Name="SpiralModulePanel" IsVisible="False" Spacing="10">
          <TextBlock Classes="module-title" Text="Spiral Overlay" />
          <TextBlock Classes="page-blurb" Text="Show hypnotic spiral overlay" />

          <!-- TWO dials, and both are read by the running effect. WPF's panel also
               carries a Randomize toggle, a Display-monitor dropdown, the SPIRAL LIBRARY card and
               a preview pane (§8.4). None of those is ported — the randomiser is D87, the monitor
               target is D66 — and rendering them dead is exactly the greyed control that swallows
               the gesture (§9 D7), so what is not here is absent rather than disabled. -->
          <CheckBox x:Name="SpiralEnableToggle"
                    Classes="dial-enable session-owned"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SpiralEnableToggle"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Spiral Overlay enabled"
                    Content="Enable Spiral Overlay" />

          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="150,*,Auto" RowDefinitions="Auto" Margin="0,4,0,0">
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Classes="dial-label" Text="Spiral opacity" />
            <Slider Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1"
                    x:Name="SpiralOpacitySlider"
                    Classes="session-owned"
                    Minimum="0" Maximum="100"
                    SmallChange="1" LargeChange="10"
                    IsSnapToTickEnabled="True" TickFrequency="1"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SpiralOpacitySlider"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Spiral opacity" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="2"
                       x:Name="SpiralOpacityValue"
                       Classes="dial-value"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SpiralOpacityValue"
                       Text="" />
          </Grid>

          <!-- THE SPIRAL LIBRARY PICKER — upstream's SPIRAL LIBRARY card
               (Features/SpiralFeatureControl.xaml:151-199, .xaml.cs:253-400), which is a picker and
               not a dial and is therefore NOT session-locked: upstream leaves it unmarked, and this
               port's scripted session never writes SpiralPath either.

               A NAMED LIST rather than upstream's wall of thumbnail cards. The port bundles no art
               (D86), so every entry is a file the USER dropped in and named; a preview pane is
               still absent and still recorded. Refresh is upstream's own button (:180) and it is
               owed for upstream's own reason: the folder can change while the app is running. There
               is no "Open folder" and no file dialog — this picker only ever offers what the
               library already holds. -->
          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="150,*,Auto" RowDefinitions="Auto" Margin="0,4,0,0">
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Classes="dial-label" Text="Spiral" />
            <ComboBox Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1"
                      x:Name="SpiralPicker"
                      HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
                      AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SpiralPicker"
                      AutomationProperties.Name="Spiral" />
            <Button Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="2"
                    x:Name="SpiralRefreshButton"
                    Classes="module-action"
                    Margin="8,0,0,0"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SpiralRefreshButton"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Refresh the spiral library"
                    Content="Refresh" />
          </Grid>

          <Border Classes="notice">
            <StackPanel Spacing="4">
              <TextBlock x:Name="SpiralLiveState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SpiralLiveState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
              <TextBlock x:Name="SpiralLibraryState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SpiralLibraryState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         Opacity="0.7"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
            </StackPanel>
          </Border>

          <Border Classes="notice">
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       x:Name="SpiralSurfaceState"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SpiralSurfaceState"
                       Text="" />
          </Border>

          <!-- The one working destination on this page. Literal, not localized
               (Features/SpiralFeatureControl.xaml:128-133); the text is the emoji-stripped form
               observed live as the button's UIA name (§8.4). Ungated and not session-locked on
               the WPF path (LoomHostService.cs:30-77; §4). -->
          <Button x:Name="LoomButton"
                  Classes="module-action"
                  AutomationProperties.AutomationId="LoomButton"
                  AutomationProperties.Name="THE LOOM — weave your own spiral"
                  Content="THE LOOM — weave your own spiral" />
        </StackPanel>

        <!-- ================= Pink Filter ================= -->
        <StackPanel x:Name="PinkFilterModulePanel" IsVisible="False" Spacing="10">
          <TextBlock Classes="module-title" Text="Pink Filter" />
          <TextBlock Classes="page-blurb" Text="A tint sits over your whole screen for as long as the session runs." />

          <!-- The whole module, in two dials, because that is the whole module: it has no schedule,
               no pool and no payload beyond a colour and an opacity. WPF's panel also carries a
               colour picker with a reset, a ramp-link checkbox and a display-monitor dropdown
               (Features/PinkFilterFeatureControl.xaml). The colour is honoured — the persisted
               string is read and parsed — but nothing here PICKS one yet, so the swatch below
               reports the tint in force rather than offering a control that does not work. -->
          <CheckBox x:Name="PinkFilterEnableToggle"
                    Classes="dial-enable session-owned"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="PinkFilterEnableToggle"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Pink Filter enabled"
                    Content="Enable Pink Filter" />

          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="150,*,Auto" RowDefinitions="Auto" Margin="0,4,0,0">
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Classes="dial-label" Text="Tint opacity" />
            <Slider Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1"
                    x:Name="PinkFilterOpacitySlider"
                    Classes="session-owned"
                    Minimum="0" Maximum="50"
                    SmallChange="1" LargeChange="5"
                    IsSnapToTickEnabled="True" TickFrequency="1"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="PinkFilterOpacitySlider"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Tint opacity" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="2"
                       x:Name="PinkFilterOpacityValue"
                       Classes="dial-value"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="PinkFilterOpacityValue"
                       Text="" />
          </Grid>

          <!-- THE TINT PICKER — upstream's colour row (Features/PinkFilterFeatureControl.xaml:90-107),
               and NOT session-locked, because upstream leaves both of its buttons unmarked: a
               session prescribes the filter's opacity, never its colour. Reset writes the empty
               string upstream writes, which is what "use the default tint" is stored as. The
               palette is bounded and StudioPickerNotices says why. -->
          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="150,*,Auto" RowDefinitions="Auto" Margin="0,4,0,0">
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Classes="dial-label" Text="Tint colour" />
            <ComboBox Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1"
                      x:Name="PinkFilterTintPicker"
                      HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
                      AutomationProperties.AutomationId="PinkFilterTintPicker"
                      AutomationProperties.Name="Tint colour" />
            <Button Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="2"
                    x:Name="PinkFilterTintResetButton"
                    Classes="module-action"
                    Margin="8,0,0,0"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="PinkFilterTintResetButton"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Reset the tint colour"
                    Content="Reset" />
          </Grid>

          <Border Classes="notice">
            <StackPanel Spacing="4">
              <TextBlock x:Name="PinkFilterLiveState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="PinkFilterLiveState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
              <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8">
                <Rectangle x:Name="PinkFilterSwatch"
                           Width="18" Height="18"
                           RadiusX="3" RadiusY="3"
                           AutomationProperties.AutomationId="PinkFilterSwatch" />
                <TextBlock x:Name="PinkFilterTintState"
                           AutomationProperties.AutomationId="PinkFilterTintState"
                           Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                           Opacity="0.7"
                           FontSize="12"
                           VerticalAlignment="Center"
                           TextWrapping="Wrap"
                           Text="" />
              </StackPanel>
            </StackPanel>
          </Border>

          <Border Classes="notice">
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       x:Name="PinkFilterSurfaceState"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="PinkFilterSurfaceState"
                       Text="" />
          </Border>
        </StackPanel>

        <!-- ================= Mind Wipe ================= -->
        <!-- THE FIRST PANEL ON THIS PAGE WHOSE MODULE THE USER HEARS. It has no surface line and
             it is not like the ramp's absence either: the ramp genuinely has no output medium,
             while this one has a medium nobody in the process can observe. So the closing notice
             is not a sentence about a platform — it is the AUDIO CAPABILITY'S own typed outcome,
             quoting what was asked of the operating system and what came back, including the peak
             level Windows measured on this process's own stream. -->
        <!-- ================= Lock Card ================= -->
        <!-- ================= Bubble Pop ================= -->
        <StackPanel x:Name="BubblePopModulePanel" IsVisible="False" Spacing="10">
          <TextBlock Classes="module-title" Text="Bubble Pop" />
          <TextBlock Classes="page-blurb"
                     Text="Floats small bubbles up your screen. Click one to pop it." />

          <!-- THE INTERRUPTION WARNING LEADS, above the dials, as the Lock Card's and Bubble
               Count's do — and this one has the OPPOSITE thing to promise: these windows are
               always on top and they take clicks, and they take NOTHING ELSE. Constants in
               PointerPanelNotices rather than inline prose so a fact can pin the exact words. -->
          <Border Classes="notice">
            <TextBlock x:Name="BubblePopInterruptionNotice"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BubblePopInterruptionNotice"
                       TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       Text="" />
          </Border>

          <!-- THREE dials, all three read by the running field. Upstream's card also carries a
               Solid-mode switch, a pop-volume slider, a ramp link, a session-clickable switch and
               the whole Trigger Bubbles block; every one of them is ABSENT rather than greyed, and
               BubblePopPresetDocument says why for each (D7 — a disabled dial swallows the
               gesture and tells the user nothing). -->
          <CheckBox x:Name="BubblePopEnableToggle"
                    Classes="dial-enable session-owned"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BubblePopEnableToggle"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Bubble Pop enabled"
                    Content="Enable Bubble Pop" />

          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="150,*,Auto" RowDefinitions="Auto,Auto,Auto" Margin="0,4,0,0">
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Classes="dial-label" Text="Bubbles per minute" />
            <Slider Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1"
                    x:Name="BubblePopFrequencySlider"
                    Classes="session-owned"
                    Minimum="1" Maximum="60"
                    SmallChange="1" LargeChange="5"
                    IsSnapToTickEnabled="True" TickFrequency="1"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BubblePopFrequencySlider"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Bubble Pop bubbles per minute" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="2"
                       x:Name="BubblePopFrequencyValue"
                       Classes="dial-value"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BubblePopFrequencyValue"
                       Text="" />

            <TextBlock Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="0" Classes="dial-label" Text="Size" />
            <Slider Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="1"
                    x:Name="BubblePopSizeSlider"
                    Minimum="50" Maximum="150"
                    SmallChange="5" LargeChange="10"
                    IsSnapToTickEnabled="True" TickFrequency="5"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BubblePopSizeSlider"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Bubble Pop size" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="2"
                       x:Name="BubblePopSizeValue"
                       Classes="dial-value"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BubblePopSizeValue"
                       Text="" />

            <TextBlock Grid.Row="2" Grid.Column="0" Classes="dial-label" Text="Extra speed" />
            <Slider Grid.Row="2" Grid.Column="1"
                    x:Name="BubblePopSpeedSlider"
                    Classes="session-owned"
                    Minimum="0" Maximum="500"
                    SmallChange="10" LargeChange="50"
                    IsSnapToTickEnabled="True" TickFrequency="10"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BubblePopSpeedSlider"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Bubble Pop extra speed" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="2" Grid.Column="2"
                       x:Name="BubblePopSpeedValue"
                       Classes="dial-value"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BubblePopSpeedValue"
                       Text="" />
          </Grid>

          <Border Classes="notice">
            <StackPanel Spacing="4">
              <TextBlock x:Name="BubblePopLiveState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BubblePopLiveState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
              <TextBlock x:Name="BubblePopDeliveryState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BubblePopDeliveryState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         Opacity="0.7"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
            </StackPanel>
          </Border>

          <Border Classes="notice">
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       x:Name="BubblePopScopeNotice"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BubblePopScopeNotice"
                       Text="" />
          </Border>

          <!-- THE EVIDENCE NOTICE. No other panel has one, and this row needs it: everything else
               on this page is something the user can SEE happening, while "a click would reach
               this window" is a question only the operating system can answer. The notice says
               what was asked and, in the same breath, what no automated check can ever show. -->
          <Border Classes="notice">
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       x:Name="BubblePopEvidenceNotice"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BubblePopEvidenceNotice"
                       Text="" />
          </Border>

          <Border Classes="notice">
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       x:Name="BubblePopCapabilityState"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BubblePopCapabilityState"
                       Text="" />
          </Border>
        </StackPanel>

        <!-- ================= Bubble Count ================= -->
        <StackPanel x:Name="BubbleCountModulePanel" IsVisible="False" Spacing="10">
          <TextBlock Classes="module-title" Text="Bubble Count" />
          <TextBlock Classes="page-blurb"
                     Text="Plays one of your clips with bubbles on it, then asks how many there were." />

          <!-- THE INTERRUPTION WARNING LEADS, above the dials, as the Lock Card's does — and it
               names BOTH interruptions, because this row covers part of the screen and then takes
               the keyboard. Constants in BubbleCountPanelNotices rather than inline prose so a fact
               can pin the exact words. -->
          <Border Classes="notice">
            <TextBlock x:Name="BubbleCountInterruptionNotice"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BubbleCountInterruptionNotice"
                       TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       Text="" />
          </Border>

          <!-- TWO dials, and both are read by the running module. WPF's panel also carries a strict
               lock; this build has neither of the two things strict mode does (no retry loop, and
               Esc is always live — D112), so it is ABSENT rather than greyed. A disabled dial
               swallows the gesture and tells the user nothing (§9 D7). -->
          <CheckBox x:Name="BubbleCountEnableToggle"
                    Classes="dial-enable session-owned"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BubbleCountEnableToggle"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Bubble Count enabled"
                    Content="Enable Bubble Count" />

          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="150,*,Auto" RowDefinitions="Auto,Auto" Margin="0,4,0,0">
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Classes="dial-label" Text="Games per hour" />
            <Slider Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1"
                    x:Name="BubbleCountFrequencySlider"
                    Classes="session-owned"
                    Minimum="1" Maximum="10"
                    SmallChange="1" LargeChange="1"
                    IsSnapToTickEnabled="True" TickFrequency="1"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BubbleCountFrequencySlider"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Bubble Count games per hour" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="2"
                       x:Name="BubbleCountFrequencyValue"
                       Classes="dial-value"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BubbleCountFrequencyValue"
                       Text="" />

            <TextBlock Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="0" Classes="dial-label" Text="Difficulty" />
            <Slider Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="1"
                    x:Name="BubbleCountDifficultySlider"
                    Classes="session-owned"
                    Minimum="0" Maximum="2"
                    SmallChange="1" LargeChange="1"
                    IsSnapToTickEnabled="True" TickFrequency="1"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BubbleCountDifficultySlider"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Bubble Count difficulty" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="2"
                       x:Name="BubbleCountDifficultyValue"
                       Classes="dial-value"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BubbleCountDifficultyValue"
                       Text="" />
          </Grid>

          <Border Classes="notice">
            <StackPanel Spacing="4">
              <TextBlock x:Name="BubbleCountLiveState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BubbleCountLiveState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
              <TextBlock x:Name="BubbleCountDifficultyState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BubbleCountDifficultyState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         Opacity="0.7"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
              <TextBlock x:Name="BubbleCountClipState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BubbleCountClipState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         Opacity="0.7"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
            </StackPanel>
          </Border>

          <Border Classes="notice">
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       x:Name="BubbleCountScopeNotice"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BubbleCountScopeNotice"
                       Text="" />
          </Border>

          <!-- BOTH capabilities' own answers, verbatim and side by side. This is the only panel on
               the page that quotes two, because it is the only row that needs two. -->
          <Border Classes="notice">
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       x:Name="BubbleCountCapabilityState"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BubbleCountCapabilityState"
                       Text="" />
          </Border>
        </StackPanel>

        <StackPanel x:Name="LockCardModulePanel" IsVisible="False" Spacing="10">
          <TextBlock Classes="module-title" Text="Lock Card" />
          <TextBlock Classes="page-blurb"
                     Text="Shows a card at random and asks you to type a phrase." />

          <!-- THE INTERRUPTION WARNING LEADS, above the dials, and it is a constant in
               InputPanelNotices rather than inline prose so a fact can pin the exact words. This
               is the only module in the rack that takes the keyboard, and a user is entitled to
               know that before they tick the box rather than the first time a card lands in the
               middle of somebody else's chat window. -->
          <Border Classes="notice">
            <TextBlock x:Name="LockCardInterruptionNotice"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="LockCardInterruptionNotice"
                       TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       Text="" />
          </Border>

          <!-- THREE dials, and all three are read by the running module. WPF's panel also carries
               a phrase editor dialog and a voice-solve switch
               (Features/LockCardFeatureControl.xaml.cs:203-210, and LockCardVoiceMode). Neither is
               ported, so both are ABSENT rather than greyed — a disabled dial swallows the gesture
               and tells the user nothing (§9 D7). -->
          <CheckBox x:Name="LockCardEnableToggle"
                    Classes="dial-enable session-owned"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="LockCardEnableToggle"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Lock Card enabled"
                    Content="Enable Lock Card" />

          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="150,*,Auto" RowDefinitions="Auto,Auto" Margin="0,4,0,0">
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Classes="dial-label" Text="Cards per hour" />
            <Slider Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1"
                    x:Name="LockCardFrequencySlider"
                    Classes="session-owned"
                    Minimum="1" Maximum="10"
                    SmallChange="1" LargeChange="1"
                    IsSnapToTickEnabled="True" TickFrequency="1"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="LockCardFrequencySlider"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Lock Card cards per hour" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="2"
                       x:Name="LockCardFrequencyValue"
                       Classes="dial-value"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="LockCardFrequencyValue"
                       Text="" />

            <TextBlock Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="0" Classes="dial-label" Text="Repeats" />
            <Slider Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="1"
                    x:Name="LockCardRepeatsSlider"
                    Classes="session-owned"
                    Minimum="1" Maximum="10"
                    SmallChange="1" LargeChange="1"
                    IsSnapToTickEnabled="True" TickFrequency="1"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="LockCardRepeatsSlider"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Lock Card repeats" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="2"
                       x:Name="LockCardRepeatsValue"
                       Classes="dial-value"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="LockCardRepeatsValue"
                       Text="" />
          </Grid>

          <CheckBox x:Name="LockCardStrictToggle"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="LockCardStrictToggle"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Lock Card strict mode"
                    Content="Strict (the card refuses Alt+F4; Esc still closes it)" />

          <Border Classes="notice">
            <StackPanel Spacing="4">
              <TextBlock x:Name="LockCardLiveState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="LockCardLiveState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
              <TextBlock x:Name="LockCardDemandState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="LockCardDemandState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         Opacity="0.7"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
              <TextBlock x:Name="LockCardPhraseState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="LockCardPhraseState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         Opacity="0.7"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
            </StackPanel>
          </Border>

          <Border Classes="notice">
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       x:Name="LockCardScopeNotice"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="LockCardScopeNotice"
                       Text="" />
          </Border>

          <Border Classes="notice">
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       x:Name="LockCardCaptureState"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="LockCardCaptureState"
                       Text="" />
          </Border>
        </StackPanel>

        <!-- ================= Pop Quiz ================= -->
        <StackPanel x:Name="PopQuizModulePanel" IsVisible="False" Spacing="10">
          <TextBlock Classes="module-title" Text="Pop Quiz" />
          <TextBlock Classes="page-blurb"
                     Text="Asks you a question at random and agrees with whatever you answer." />

          <!-- THE INTERRUPTION WARNING LEADS, above the dials, as the Lock Card's and Bubble
               Count's do — this row takes the keyboard. Constants in PopQuizPanelNotices rather
               than inline prose so a fact can pin the exact words. -->
          <Border Classes="notice">
            <TextBlock x:Name="PopQuizInterruptionNotice"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="PopQuizInterruptionNotice"
                       TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       Text="" />
          </Border>

          <!-- TWO dials, because upstream has exactly two: ChkPopQuizEnabled and
               SliderPopQuizFrequency (Views/Tabs/GradedIntakeTabView.xaml:268-292). There is no
               strict mode, no colour, no repeat count and no phrase list to carry.

               NEITHER IS `session-owned`, AND THAT IS THE RESOLUTION OF A REAL CONFLICT — read
               the SessionOwnedMarker doc comment in StudioPage.axaml.cs before adding the class
               here. Upstream marks BOTH of these Owned (:269, :286) because upstream's run takes
               CUSTODY of the two values (Services/Session/SessionEngine.cs:919-920 saves them, :1544-1545 writes
               them back). This port's run borrows eleven documents and this is not one of them,
               so nothing is discarded at a restore and greying them would take control away for
               a harm this build does not have. -->
          <CheckBox x:Name="PopQuizEnableToggle"
                    Classes="dial-enable"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="PopQuizEnableToggle"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Pop Quiz enabled"
                    Content="Enable Pop Quiz" />

          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="150,*,Auto" RowDefinitions="Auto" Margin="0,4,0,0">
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Classes="dial-label" Text="Questions per hour" />
            <!-- Minimum/Maximum are upstream's slider's own, 1 and 100
                 (GradedIntakeTabView.xaml:286), which is also AppSettings' clamp
                 (Models/AppSettings.cs:3586) — see PopQuizSchedule.MaxPerHour for why the stale
                 "1-10" comment beside it does not win. -->
            <Slider Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1"
                    x:Name="PopQuizFrequencySlider"
                    Minimum="1" Maximum="100"
                    SmallChange="1" LargeChange="1"
                    IsSnapToTickEnabled="True" TickFrequency="1"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="PopQuizFrequencySlider"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Pop Quiz questions per hour" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="2"
                       x:Name="PopQuizFrequencyValue"
                       Classes="dial-value"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="PopQuizFrequencyValue"
                       Text="" />
          </Grid>

          <Border Classes="notice">
            <StackPanel Spacing="4">
              <TextBlock x:Name="PopQuizLiveState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="PopQuizLiveState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
              <TextBlock x:Name="PopQuizPoolState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="PopQuizPoolState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         Opacity="0.7"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
              <TextBlock x:Name="PopQuizXpState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="PopQuizXpState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         Opacity="0.7"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
            </StackPanel>
          </Border>

          <Border Classes="notice">
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       x:Name="PopQuizScopeNotice"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="PopQuizScopeNotice"
                       Text="" />
          </Border>

          <!-- The missing Test button, named rather than stubbed. See
               PopQuizPanelNotices.NoTestButtonNotice. -->
          <Border Classes="notice">
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       x:Name="PopQuizTestNotice"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="PopQuizTestNotice"
                       Text="" />
          </Border>

          <Border Classes="notice">
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       x:Name="PopQuizCapabilityState"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="PopQuizCapabilityState"
                       Text="" />
          </Border>
        </StackPanel>

        <StackPanel x:Name="MindWipeModulePanel" IsVisible="False" Spacing="10">
          <TextBlock Classes="module-title" Text="Mind Wipe" />
          <TextBlock Classes="page-blurb" Text="Plays a short clip at random while the session runs." />

          <!-- TWO dials, and both are read by the running effect. WPF's panel also carries loop
               mode with its A/B crossfade engine, a custom-clip file picker and a test button
               (MindWipeService.cs:293-380, :139-146, :879-895). None is ported, so all three are
               ABSENT rather than greyed — a disabled dial swallows the gesture and tells the user
               nothing (§9 D7). -->
          <CheckBox x:Name="MindWipeEnableToggle"
                    Classes="dial-enable session-owned"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="MindWipeEnableToggle"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Mind Wipe enabled"
                    Content="Enable Mind Wipe" />

          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="150,*,Auto" RowDefinitions="Auto,Auto" Margin="0,4,0,0">
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Classes="dial-label" Text="Clips per hour" />
            <Slider Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1"
                    x:Name="MindWipeFrequencySlider"
                    Classes="session-owned"
                    Minimum="1" Maximum="180"
                    SmallChange="1" LargeChange="10"
                    IsSnapToTickEnabled="True" TickFrequency="1"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="MindWipeFrequencySlider"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Mind Wipe clips per hour" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="2"
                       x:Name="MindWipeFrequencyValue"
                       Classes="dial-value"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="MindWipeFrequencyValue"
                       Text="" />

            <TextBlock Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="0" Classes="dial-label" Text="Volume" />
            <Slider Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="1"
                    x:Name="MindWipeVolumeSlider"
                    Minimum="0" Maximum="100"
                    SmallChange="1" LargeChange="10"
                    IsSnapToTickEnabled="True" TickFrequency="1"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="MindWipeVolumeSlider"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Mind Wipe volume" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="2"
                       x:Name="MindWipeVolumeValue"
                       Classes="dial-value"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="MindWipeVolumeValue"
                       Text="" />
          </Grid>

          <Border Classes="notice">
            <StackPanel Spacing="4">
              <TextBlock x:Name="MindWipeLiveState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="MindWipeLiveState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
              <TextBlock x:Name="MindWipeClipState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="MindWipeClipState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         Opacity="0.7"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
            </StackPanel>
          </Border>

          <Border Classes="notice">
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       x:Name="MindWipeAudioState"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="MindWipeAudioState"
                       Text="" />
          </Border>
        </StackPanel>

        <!-- ================= Brain Drain (audio half) ================= -->
        <!-- THE ONLY PANEL ON THIS PAGE FOR HALF A ROW. Its missing half is stated in three
             places on purpose — the row's own title, the permanent notice below, and the arm
             result's typed reason (braindrain-visual-half-absent, which this module returns on
             EVERY run however healthy). A half-row whose absent half is only visible in a record
             file is a half-row that looks complete. -->
        <StackPanel x:Name="BrainDrainModulePanel" IsVisible="False" Spacing="10">
          <TextBlock Classes="module-title" Text="Brain Drain (audio half)" />
          <TextBlock Classes="page-blurb" Text="Plays a short clip at random while the session runs." />

          <!-- THE MISSING HALF, first thing on the panel and not buried at the bottom. Its text is
               the module's own constant (BrainDrainEffect.VisualHalfNotice), which is the same
               string the arm result carries, so the panel and the typed outcome cannot drift into
               two different accounts of one absence. -->
          <Border Classes="notice">
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       x:Name="BrainDrainVisualHalfState"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BrainDrainVisualHalfState"
                       Text="" />
          </Border>

          <CheckBox x:Name="BrainDrainEnableToggle"
                    Classes="dial-enable"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BrainDrainEnableToggle"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Brain Drain audio half enabled"
                    Content="Enable Brain Drain (audio half)" />

          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="150,*,Auto" RowDefinitions="Auto,Auto" Margin="0,4,0,0">
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Classes="dial-label" Text="Intensity" />
            <Slider Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1"
                    x:Name="BrainDrainIntensitySlider"
                    Minimum="1" Maximum="100"
                    SmallChange="1" LargeChange="10"
                    IsSnapToTickEnabled="True" TickFrequency="1"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BrainDrainIntensitySlider"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Brain Drain intensity" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="2"
                       x:Name="BrainDrainIntensityValue"
                       Classes="dial-value"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BrainDrainIntensityValue"
                       Text="" />

            <TextBlock Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="0" Classes="dial-label" Text="Volume" />
            <Slider Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="1"
                    x:Name="BrainDrainVolumeSlider"
                    Minimum="0" Maximum="100"
                    SmallChange="1" LargeChange="10"
                    IsSnapToTickEnabled="True" TickFrequency="1"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BrainDrainVolumeSlider"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Brain Drain volume" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="2"
                       x:Name="BrainDrainVolumeValue"
                       Classes="dial-value"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BrainDrainVolumeValue"
                       Text="" />
          </Grid>

          <!-- Upstream's own switch (BrainDrainService.cs:60-69). Here it changes ONLY the audio
               grain; upstream's same flag also doubles the blur's capture rate from 30 to 60 FPS
               (OverlayService.cs:1986-1987), which is the half that is not here. -->
          <CheckBox x:Name="BrainDrainHighRefreshToggle"
                    Classes="dial-enable"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BrainDrainHighRefreshToggle"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Brain Drain high refresh"
                    Content="High refresh (roll every 0.5s instead of every 5s)" />

          <Border Classes="notice">
            <StackPanel Spacing="4">
              <TextBlock x:Name="BrainDrainLiveState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BrainDrainLiveState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
              <TextBlock x:Name="BrainDrainClipState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BrainDrainClipState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         Opacity="0.7"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
            </StackPanel>
          </Border>

          <Border Classes="notice">
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       x:Name="BrainDrainAudioState"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BrainDrainAudioState"
                       Text="" />
          </Border>
        </StackPanel>

        <!-- ================= Haptics ================= -->
        <!-- THREE CONTROLS, and the count is the finding rather than an omission. Upstream's haptics
             page is 1640 lines over a 9193-line service (Views/Tabs/HapticsTabView.xaml,
             Services/Haptics/**): two provider boxes with their own URLs, an auto-connect box, a
             per-event routing table, a master cap, a temperament and a DSP block. Every one of THOSE
             configures a mixer or an address this build has no implementation for, so every one of
             them would be a control that decides nothing (§9 D7).

             THE TWO PROVIDER BOXES ARE HERE BECAUSE THEY NOW DECIDE SOMETHING. Both of upstream's
             real routes have a client (HapticSinkFactory.AdmittedRoutes) and both flags default
             FALSE, which is upstream's own stored default (Models/HapticSettings.cs:769). Without
             these boxes that default would be unreachable-by-design: the nothing-ticked refusal
             names a checkbox, and it must not name one that does not exist.

             CHECKBOXES, NEVER A DROPDOWN, and that is upstream's shape rather than a layout choice:
             the v2 manager connects every enabled provider concurrently and succeeds if any did
             (HapticDeviceManager.cs:101-125), and upstream's own header says so —
             "PROVIDERS - enable as many as you like, they connect together" (en.json:4113,
             HapticsTabView.xaml:726). The URL boxes beside upstream's are NOT ported: this build
             has no setting that honours a custom address.

             NOT GATED BY THE PREMIUM CHECK, which is also upstream's: ChkHapticProvider_Changed
             writes and saves with no gate (MainWindow/MainWindow.Haptics.cs:580-595); the gate is on
             the master toggle (:552-564) and the connect button (:633-641). A ticked route on its
             own reaches nothing.

             NO SURFACE LINE OF THE USUAL KIND, and a capability line instead. Every drawing
             module's panel ends with "where did the picture go"; this module's output is not on
             this screen and not in this process at all — it is in another program, over loopback —
             so what it reports is what the sink last said about that.

             THE GATE'S ANSWER IS ITS OWN LINE, and it is never merged with the capability line. The
             Windows app shows one modal for both ("Haptic feedback is available for Patreon
             supporters.", en.json:3394, MainWindow/MainWindow.Haptics.cs:492-496) whether the answer
             was "no pledge" or "I could not ask". Those are different facts about different things
             and this panel says which. -->
        <StackPanel x:Name="HapticsModulePanel" IsVisible="False" Spacing="10">
          <TextBlock Classes="module-title" Text="Haptics" />
          <TextBlock Classes="page-blurb"
                     x:Name="HapticsWhatItIs"
                     TextWrapping="Wrap"
                     AutomationProperties.AutomationId="HapticsWhatItIs"
                     Text="" />

          <CheckBox x:Name="HapticsEnableToggle"
                    Classes="dial-enable"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="HapticsEnableToggle"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Haptics enabled"
                    Content="Enable Haptics" />

          <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="18">
            <CheckBox x:Name="HapticsLovenseToggle"
                      AutomationProperties.AutomationId="HapticsLovenseToggle"
                      AutomationProperties.Name="Lovense provider enabled"
                      ToolTip.Tip="Needs Lovense Connect or Lovense Remote running on this machine."
                      Content="Lovense" />
            <CheckBox x:Name="HapticsButtplugToggle"
                      AutomationProperties.AutomationId="HapticsButtplugToggle"
                      AutomationProperties.Name="Intiface provider enabled"
                      ToolTip.Tip="Needs Intiface Central or Intiface Engine running on this machine."
                      Content="Intiface (Buttplug.io)" />
          </StackPanel>

          <Border Classes="notice">
            <StackPanel Spacing="4">
              <TextBlock x:Name="HapticsLiveState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="HapticsLiveState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
              <TextBlock x:Name="HapticsGateState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="HapticsGateState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
              <TextBlock x:Name="HapticsSinkState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="HapticsSinkState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         Opacity="0.7"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
            </StackPanel>
          </Border>

          <Border Classes="notice">
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       x:Name="HapticsAbsenceState"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="HapticsAbsenceState"
                       Text="" />
          </Border>
        </StackPanel>

        <!-- ================= Audio ================= -->
        <!-- FOUR CONTROLS, AND THEY ARE UPSTREAM'S FOUR: master volume, video volume, the output
             endpoint picker with its Refresh, and Test audio
             (Views/Controls/AppSettings/AudioSettingsSection.xaml:82-89, :103-110, :181-190,
             :195-201). Upstream's card carries three more — the auto-duck switch, the duck amount
             and the don't-duck-the-browser exception (:114-167) — and none of them is here, because
             cross-application ducking is a pending owner platform decision this build refuses
             TYPED rather than pretends (Audio/AudioSeams.UnavailableDuckSink, composed by
             AudioParticipant). A duck slider over a sink that always answers "not applied" is the
             dead dial §9 D7 refuses. Its Audio Layers button (:202-207) opens a mixer window this
             port does not have.

             WHEN THE ENDPOINTS ARE ENUMERATED, AND WHY IT IS NOT AT PAGE LOAD. This page and all
             seventeen of its panels are built once, during startup, so "on load" here means "at
             every launch". Enumerating there would build a native miniaudio context for a user who
             never opens this row and never plays a sound — the same class of unrequested claim on a
             shared resource that AudioParticipant's phase 3 exists to refuse, and the reason
             EnsureDevice is deferred to the first consumer that needs sound. So the list is read on
             the FIRST REVEAL of this panel and again on Refresh, which is also where upstream reads
             it (MainWindow/MainWindow.Settings.cs:140, inside LoadSettings, when the audio door's
             own state loads). Listing is not opening: EnumerateDevices reads the engine's playback
             devices and only TryInit brings a device up (Audio/SoundFlowAudioBackend.cs:58-70 vs
             :73-110), so the picker can offer endpoints without seizing one.

             THE SLIDERS ARE NOT session-owned, AND NEITHER IS ANYTHING ELSE HERE. See the row's own
             comment above and the SessionOwnedMarker remarks in StudioPage.axaml.cs. -->
        <StackPanel x:Name="AudioModulePanel" IsVisible="False" Spacing="10">
          <TextBlock Classes="module-title" Text="Audio" />
          <TextBlock Classes="page-blurb"
                     x:Name="AudioWhatItIs"
                     TextWrapping="Wrap"
                     AutomationProperties.AutomationId="AudioWhatItIs"
                     Text="" />

          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="150,*,Auto" RowDefinitions="Auto,Auto" Margin="0,4,0,0">
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Classes="dial-label" Text="Master volume" />
            <Slider Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1"
                    x:Name="AudioMasterSlider"
                    Minimum="0" Maximum="100"
                    SmallChange="1" LargeChange="10"
                    IsSnapToTickEnabled="True" TickFrequency="1"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="AudioMasterSlider"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Master volume" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="2"
                       x:Name="AudioMasterValue"
                       Classes="dial-value"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="AudioMasterValue"
                       Text="" />

            <TextBlock Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="0" Classes="dial-label" Text="Video volume" />
            <Slider Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="1"
                    x:Name="AudioVideoSlider"
                    Minimum="0" Maximum="100"
                    SmallChange="1" LargeChange="10"
                    IsSnapToTickEnabled="True" TickFrequency="1"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="AudioVideoSlider"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Video volume" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="2"
                       x:Name="AudioVideoValue"
                       Classes="dial-value"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="AudioVideoValue"
                       Text="" />
          </Grid>

          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="150,*,Auto">
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Classes="dial-label" Text="Output" />
            <ComboBox Grid.Column="1"
                      x:Name="AudioDevicePicker"
                      HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
                      AutomationProperties.AutomationId="AudioDevicePicker"
                      AutomationProperties.Name="Audio output device" />
            <Button Grid.Column="2"
                    x:Name="AudioDeviceRefreshButton"
                    Classes="module-action"
                    Margin="8,0,0,0"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="AudioDeviceRefreshButton"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Refresh the list of audio outputs"
                    Content="Refresh" />
          </Grid>

          <!-- THE ONE CONTROL HERE WHOSE POINT IS THAT A HUMAN HEARS SOMETHING. It brings a device
               up for real and cues a real clip at a fixed half gain that deliberately ignores the
               master dial (Services/AudioService.cs:625). It refuses BY NAME when there is no clip
               to cue, which is upstream's own branch (:600-607) rather than a port shortfall
               dressed up as one. -->
          <Button x:Name="AudioTestButton"
                  Classes="module-action"
                  HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                  AutomationProperties.AutomationId="AudioTestButton"
                  AutomationProperties.Name="Test audio"
                  Content="Test audio" />

          <Border Classes="notice">
            <StackPanel Spacing="4">
              <TextBlock x:Name="AudioMasterState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="AudioMasterState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
              <TextBlock x:Name="AudioVideoState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="AudioVideoState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
              <TextBlock x:Name="AudioChoiceState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="AudioChoiceState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
              <TextBlock x:Name="AudioDeviceState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="AudioDeviceState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         Opacity="0.7"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
            </StackPanel>
          </Border>

          <Border Classes="notice">
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       x:Name="AudioTestState"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="AudioTestState"
                       Text="" />
          </Border>
        </StackPanel>

        <!-- ================= Scheduler ================= -->
        <!-- TEN CONTROLS, WHICH IS UPSTREAM'S TEN: an enable, two times and seven day boxes
             (Features/SchedulerFeatureControl.xaml, loaded at .xaml.cs:42-51). The four other
             Scheduler* settings on AppSettings (:2461-2478) belong to the intensity RAMP and are
             on no scheduler surface upstream either.

             THE TIMES ARE FREE TEXT, deliberately, and that is upstream's control
             (SchedulerFeatureControl.xaml:47,56 are TextBoxes written on LostFocus, :49/:58).
             A picker would make the unparseable-time fallback unreachable — and that fallback is
             a live behaviour: a time this build cannot read silently becomes 16:00 or 22:00
             (MainWindow/MainWindow.StartStop.cs:667-677). The panel says so out loud instead,
             because the alternative is a session starting at an hour nobody chose.

             NO SURFACE LINE. Like the Intensity Ramp below it, this module acquires no
             capability at all; a sentence about where its pixels went would be about something
             it never asks for. -->
        <StackPanel x:Name="SchedulerModulePanel" IsVisible="False" Spacing="10">
          <TextBlock Classes="module-title" Text="Scheduler" />
          <TextBlock Classes="page-blurb"
                     x:Name="SchedulerWhatItIs"
                     TextWrapping="Wrap"
                     AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SchedulerWhatItIs"
                     Text="" />

          <CheckBox x:Name="SchedulerEnableToggle"
                    Classes="dial-enable"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SchedulerEnableToggle"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Scheduler enabled"
                    Content="Enable Scheduler" />

          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="150,Auto,Auto,Auto" RowDefinitions="Auto" Margin="0,4,0,0">
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Classes="dial-label" Text="Time range" />
            <TextBox Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1"
                     x:Name="SchedulerStartTimeBox"
                     Width="80"
                     AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SchedulerStartTimeBox"
                     AutomationProperties.Name="Scheduled start time" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="2"
                       Classes="dial-label"
                       Margin="10,0"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center"
                       Text="to" />
            <TextBox Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="3"
                     x:Name="SchedulerEndTimeBox"
                     Width="80"
                     AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SchedulerEndTimeBox"
                     AutomationProperties.Name="Scheduled end time" />
          </Grid>

          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="150,*" RowDefinitions="Auto" Margin="0,4,0,0">
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Classes="dial-label" Text="Active days" />
            <StackPanel Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1" Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="10">
              <CheckBox x:Name="SchedulerDayMon" Content="M"
                        AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SchedulerDayMon"
                        AutomationProperties.Name="Scheduler active on Monday" />
              <CheckBox x:Name="SchedulerDayTue" Content="T"
                        AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SchedulerDayTue"
                        AutomationProperties.Name="Scheduler active on Tuesday" />
              <CheckBox x:Name="SchedulerDayWed" Content="W"
                        AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SchedulerDayWed"
                        AutomationProperties.Name="Scheduler active on Wednesday" />
              <CheckBox x:Name="SchedulerDayThu" Content="T"
                        AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SchedulerDayThu"
                        AutomationProperties.Name="Scheduler active on Thursday" />
              <CheckBox x:Name="SchedulerDayFri" Content="F"
                        AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SchedulerDayFri"
                        AutomationProperties.Name="Scheduler active on Friday" />
              <CheckBox x:Name="SchedulerDaySat" Content="S"
                        AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SchedulerDaySat"
                        AutomationProperties.Name="Scheduler active on Saturday" />
              <CheckBox x:Name="SchedulerDaySun" Content="S"
                        AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SchedulerDaySun"
                        AutomationProperties.Name="Scheduler active on Sunday" />
            </StackPanel>
          </Grid>

          <Border Classes="notice">
            <StackPanel Spacing="4">
              <TextBlock x:Name="SchedulerLiveState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SchedulerLiveState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
              <TextBlock x:Name="SchedulerReadingState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SchedulerReadingState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
              <TextBlock x:Name="SchedulerLastTickState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SchedulerLastTickState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         Opacity="0.7"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
            </StackPanel>
          </Border>

          <Border Classes="notice">
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       x:Name="SchedulerAbsenceState"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="SchedulerAbsenceState"
                       Text="" />
          </Border>
        </StackPanel>

        <!-- ================= Intensity Ramp ================= -->
        <!-- THE FIRST PANEL ON THIS PAGE WITH NO SURFACE LINE, and its absence is the point.
             Every module before it ends in a "where did it go on screen" notice because every
             module before it drew something. This one draws nothing at all: its output is the
             OTHER modules' opacity numbers, so what it reports instead is what it is holding
             and what it will give back. A surface line here would be a sentence about a
             capability this module deliberately never acquires. -->
        <StackPanel x:Name="RampModulePanel" IsVisible="False" Spacing="10">
          <TextBlock Classes="module-title" Text="Intensity Ramp" />
          <TextBlock Classes="page-blurb"
                     Text="Turns the effects you link to it up as the session goes on, and puts them back where you left them when it stops." />

          <CheckBox x:Name="RampEnableToggle"
                    Classes="dial-enable"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="RampEnableToggle"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Intensity Ramp enabled"
                    Content="Enable Intensity Ramp" />

          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="150,*,Auto" RowDefinitions="Auto" Margin="0,4,0,0">
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Classes="dial-label" Text="Duration" />
            <Slider Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1"
                    x:Name="RampDurationSlider"
                    Minimum="10" Maximum="180"
                    SmallChange="1" LargeChange="10"
                    IsSnapToTickEnabled="True" TickFrequency="1"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="RampDurationSlider"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Ramp duration" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="2"
                       x:Name="RampDurationValue"
                       Classes="dial-value"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="RampDurationValue"
                       Text="" />
          </Grid>

          <!-- Minimum 1, not 0: this dial can only ever make an effect stronger
               (AppSettings.cs:2471 clamps to [1.0, 3.0]), and it SHIPS at 1.0 — a ramp that
               changes nothing until the user moves it. -->
          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="150,*,Auto" RowDefinitions="Auto">
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Classes="dial-label" Text="Multiplier" />
            <Slider Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1"
                    x:Name="RampMultiplierSlider"
                    Minimum="1" Maximum="3"
                    SmallChange="0.1" LargeChange="0.5"
                    IsSnapToTickEnabled="True" TickFrequency="0.1"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="RampMultiplierSlider"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Ramp multiplier" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="2"
                       x:Name="RampMultiplierValue"
                       Classes="dial-value"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="RampMultiplierValue"
                       Text="" />
          </Grid>

          <!-- The five shapes, in the enum's own order, with upstream's own wording
               (en.json:1458-1462). -->
          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="150,*" RowDefinitions="Auto">
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Classes="dial-label" Text="Ramp curve" />
            <ComboBox Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1"
                      x:Name="RampCurvePicker"
                      Classes="session-owned"
                      HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
                      AutomationProperties.AutomationId="RampCurvePicker"
                      AutomationProperties.Name="Ramp curve">
              <ComboBoxItem Content="Linear" />
              <ComboBoxItem Content="Ease In (slow start)" />
              <ComboBoxItem Content="Ease Out (fast start)" />
              <ComboBoxItem Content="S-Curve (smooth)" />
              <ComboBoxItem Content="Exponential (late surge)" />
            </ComboBox>
          </Grid>

          <CheckBox x:Name="RampEndSessionToggle"
                    Classes="dial-enable"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="RampEndSessionToggle"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="End the session when the ramp finishes"
                    Content="End the session when the ramp finishes" />

          <!-- THREE links where WPF has five (CCP.Core/Models/AppSettings.cs:2589-2621), and the
               third arrived with the Visuals row rather than being invented: D93's rule is that a link whose
               dial has no ported panel is ABSENT rather than present-and-inert, and the Visuals row
               IS flash opacity's panel. Master volume and subliminal volume still have no dial
               anywhere, so their switches are still absent — a switch that quietly does nothing is
               the greyed control §9 D7 refuses. -->
          <TextBlock Classes="dial-label" Text="Link to ramp" Margin="0,4,0,0" />
          <CheckBox x:Name="RampLinkSpiralToggle"
                    Classes="dial-enable"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="RampLinkSpiralToggle"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Link Spiral Overlay opacity to the ramp"
                    Content="Spiral Overlay opacity" />
          <CheckBox x:Name="RampLinkPinkFilterToggle"
                    Classes="dial-enable"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="RampLinkPinkFilterToggle"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Link Pink Filter opacity to the ramp"
                    Content="Pink Filter opacity" />
          <CheckBox x:Name="RampLinkFlashToggle"
                    Classes="dial-enable"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="RampLinkFlashToggle"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Link Flash Images opacity to the ramp"
                    Content="Flash Images opacity (reaches the next flash, not one already up)" />

          <Border Classes="notice">
            <StackPanel Spacing="4">
              <TextBlock x:Name="RampLiveState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="RampLiveState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
              <TextBlock x:Name="RampCustodyState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="RampCustodyState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         Opacity="0.7"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
            </StackPanel>
          </Border>
        </StackPanel>

        <!-- ================= Scripted Sessions ================= -->
        <!-- Upstream's PRESETS tab, folded into this door's rack (the row's own comment above
             carries the door evidence). What a user can do here is upstream's ceremony and no more:
             pick one of the four shipped sessions, read what it is, press one button, confirm what
             it is about to do to your settings, and watch it run.

             THE ROWS ARE BUILT IN CODE, from the four .session.json files beside the binary
             (Session/ScriptedSession.ReadBuiltIns) — as upstream builds its own from the registry
             rather than from markup, for upstream's own stated reason: "NOT MVVM, deliberately:
             this page is code-built UI ... and an ItemsControl here would need a view-model layer
             that nothing else on the page has" (MainWindow/MainWindow.SessionIO.cs:177-178,
             :212-232). The port's reason is narrower and its own: the SET of rows is data on disk,
             so markup would be a fifth copy of it that could disagree with the files. -->
        <StackPanel x:Name="ScriptedSessionModulePanel" IsVisible="False" Spacing="10">
          <TextBlock Classes="module-title" Text="Scripted Sessions" />
          <TextBlock Classes="page-blurb"
                     Text="A scripted session runs a timeline for you. It borrows your dials for its length, moves them through named phases, and gives them back when it ends." />

          <!-- THE RACK TOOLBAR, and upstream's own rule for it: "every control here is a VIEW over
               the same list - none of them mutate a session" (Views/Tabs/PresetsTabView.xaml:789-790).
               Four difficulty filters, an order, and a search; every one of them re-runs the same
               repaint and nothing else.

               THE CHIPS AND THE SORT ENTRIES ARE FILLED IN CODE, for upstream's own reason and one
               more of this port's: upstream builds its chips in BuildSessionRackToolbar because each
               carries a live count and "a count in XAML is a binding to a view-model this page
               deliberately does not have" (:793-798); here the captions and the order labels are
               derived from ScriptedSessionRack.SortLabel and SessionRackNotices.Difficulty, so a band
               or an order added to either enum cannot leave a stale word in markup behind it.

               UPSTREAM'S SOURCE CHIPS ARE NOT HERE - All / Built-in / Yours / Catalogue
               (Views/Tabs/PresetsTabView.xaml:814-816). They filter a provenance this build does not
               have - every session in this rack is built-in, custom and imported sessions are
               unported - so four chips of which three would always read 0 would be the
               fake-available shape §9 D7 bans, on the surface whose own notice names those
               absences a few lines below.

               NOTHING HERE PERSISTS, and upstream persists two of them
               (AppSettings.SessionRackSourceFilter / SessionRackSort, :800-801). Upstream keeps its
               difficulty dots and its search box session-scoped on purpose - "a filter you cannot
               see the cause of, restored a week later, reads as 'my sessions are gone'" (:802-803) -
               and the sort follows them here rather than opening a twelfth persisted document on a
               page that already holds eleven for a running session. -->
          <!-- TWO LINES WHERE UPSTREAM HAS ONE, and the reason is measured rather than aesthetic.
               Upstream's toolbar is a single 28px strip (Views/Tabs/PresetsTabView.xaml:805) across
               a rack that owns most of a 1280px tab; this rack lives in the Studio door's module
               column, which is roughly 690 DIP. On one line the four bands, the order, the search
               and the count come to more than that, and the count is what loses: a headless fact
               asserting the strip's controls have a non-zero width caught it arranged at 0 DIP
               wide — present, correct, and invisible. Splitting the strip is what keeps every
               control on it at its own size instead of silently starving one. -->
          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" RowDefinitions="Auto,Auto" Margin="0,2,0,0">
            <StackPanel Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0"
                        x:Name="ScriptedSessionFilterPanel"
                        Orientation="Horizontal"
                        Spacing="10"
                        VerticalAlignment="Center" />

            <!-- Upstream's TxtRackCount, in upstream's place: at the right of the strip above the
                 rows (MainWindow/MainWindow.SessionIO.cs:744-746, Views/Tabs/PresetsTabView.xaml:782-783).
                 In the AUTO column, so it takes its own width first and the bands take what is
                 left — the opposite of the arrangement that starved it. -->
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1"
                       x:Name="ScriptedSessionRackCount"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="ScriptedSessionRackCount"
                       Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                       Opacity="0.7"
                       FontSize="11"
                       Margin="10,0,0,0"
                       HorizontalAlignment="Right"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center"
                       Text="" />

            <StackPanel Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="0" Grid.ColumnSpan="2"
                        Orientation="Horizontal"
                        Spacing="8"
                        Margin="0,6,0,0">
              <ComboBox x:Name="ScriptedSessionSortBox"
                        Width="150"
                        VerticalAlignment="Center"
                        AutomationProperties.AutomationId="ScriptedSessionSortBox"
                        AutomationProperties.Name="Session order"
                        ToolTip.Tip="Change the order of the session list" />

              <!-- The watermark is the TextBox's own PlaceholderText, where upstream needs a sibling
                   TextBlock and a handler to hide it because WPF has none
                   (Views/Tabs/PresetsTabView.xaml:836-848). -->
              <TextBox x:Name="ScriptedSessionSearchBox"
                       Width="150"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="ScriptedSessionSearchBox"
                       AutomationProperties.Name="Search sessions" />
            </StackPanel>
          </Grid>

          <!-- ONE panel, and nothing else may add a child to it — upstream's rule for its own rack
               (MainWindow.SessionIO.cs:854-857) and the reason the repaint can be idempotent. -->
          <StackPanel x:Name="ScriptedSessionRackPanel" Spacing="2" />

          <!-- THE CONFIRMATION, and it is not decoration: it is where the app tells the user what
               it is about to do to their settings before it does it
               (MainWindow/MainWindow.Presets.cs:1465-1471). It is an inline strip rather than a
               modal window because this port has no dialog surface and a whole new window class
               would be a bigger claim than the ceremony needs; the ceremony is that the start is
               REFUSED until a second, separate gesture confirms it, and that is what this is. -->
          <Border Classes="notice" x:Name="ScriptedSessionConfirmPanel" IsVisible="False">
            <StackPanel Spacing="6">
              <TextBlock x:Name="ScriptedSessionConfirmTitle"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="ScriptedSessionConfirmTitle"
                         Classes="module-title"
                         FontSize="14"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
              <TextBlock x:Name="ScriptedSessionConfirmDetail"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="ScriptedSessionConfirmDetail"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
              <TextBlock x:Name="ScriptedSessionConfirmPromise"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="ScriptedSessionConfirmPromise"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
              <TextBlock x:Name="ScriptedSessionConfirmQuestion"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="ScriptedSessionConfirmQuestion"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         Opacity="0.7"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
              <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8">
                <Button x:Name="ScriptedSessionConfirmButton"
                        Classes="module-action"
                        HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                        AutomationProperties.AutomationId="ScriptedSessionConfirmButton"
                        AutomationProperties.Name="Start Session"
                        Content="Start Session" />
                <Button x:Name="ScriptedSessionCancelButton"
                        Classes="module-action"
                        HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                        AutomationProperties.AutomationId="ScriptedSessionCancelButton"
                        AutomationProperties.Name="Not yet"
                        Content="Not yet" />
              </StackPanel>
            </StackPanel>
          </Border>

          <!-- ONE button in two states, never disabled and never duplicated — upstream's own
               shape, and upstream says why in its own words at the top of the handler: "The button
               doubles as Start/Stop — state dictates which path to run. This also makes us
               resilient to any stale/duplicate Click subscriptions"
               (MainWindow/MainWindow.Presets.cs:1455-1459). It wears the shell's session livery
               for the same reason the shell's does: WPF paints the running one red
               (MainWindow.StartStop.cs:756) and captions it STOP SESSION with the time left
               (en.json:2321, MainWindow.Presets.cs:1752). -->
          <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="10">
            <Button x:Name="ScriptedSessionStartButton"
                    Classes="session-start"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="ScriptedSessionStartButton"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Start Session"
                    Content="Start Session" />

            <!-- PAUSE / RESUME — upstream's own second button, and upstream's own visibility rule:
                 it exists only while a session is running (MainWindow/MainWindow.Presets.cs:1809
                 shows it at start, :1855 collapses it at the end; MainWindow.xaml:2606-2607 declares
                 it Collapsed). One button in two states like the START one beside it, because
                 upstream's is one button whose glyph flips between ⏸ and ▶ (:1921, :1937). Pausing
                 asks first and resuming does not (:1919-1940) — the ask names what the pause costs,
                 which is the only reason upstream gates one direction and not the other. -->
            <Button x:Name="ScriptedSessionPauseButton"
                    Classes="module-action"
                    IsVisible="False"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                    VerticalAlignment="Center"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="ScriptedSessionPauseButton"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Pause"
                    Content="Pause" />

            <!-- EDIT SESSION — upstream's rack row action (MainWindow/MainWindow.SessionIO.cs:538
                 builds it as a ✎ on every row, :1819-1868 handles it). It is a strip button acting
                 on the SELECTION rather than a per-row hover action, because this rack's rows are
                 RadioButtons whose whole job is to carry a selection: a button inside a RadioButton
                 is a click target that would have to swallow the gesture that selects the row it is
                 in, and the port already routes Start, Stop and Pause through the same selection.

                 IT IS OFFERED FOR A BUILT-IN TOO, which is upstream's rule (:538 is outside the
                 non-built-in branch at :540) and is the ONLY way the copy rule is reachable: a user
                 with four shipped sessions and no editable ones would never be able to make a first
                 custom session. Saving one makes a copy and never touches the shipped file
                 (Session/SessionEditorRules.cs, Session/CustomSessionStore.cs). -->
            <Button x:Name="ScriptedSessionEditButton"
                    Classes="module-action"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                    VerticalAlignment="Center"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="ScriptedSessionEditButton"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Edit session"
                    ToolTip.Tip="Change the selected session's name, length and description"
                    Content="Edit session" />

            <!-- RECENT SESSIONS — upstream's own door button, on this door because the rack is
                 (MainWindow/MainWindow.Presets.cs:1440 -> SessionLogHistoryWindow;
                 Views/Tabs/PresetsTabView.xaml:646-651, where it sits in the same header strip as
                 the rack it belongs to). It opens the last twenty retained logs and reopens any
                 one of them in the same recap window a session's own end raises. -->
            <Button x:Name="ScriptedSessionHistoryButton"
                    Classes="module-action"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                    VerticalAlignment="Center"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="ScriptedSessionHistoryButton"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Recent sessions"
                    ToolTip.Tip="Browse the last 20 sessions and the videos/images each one played"
                    Content="Recent sessions" />
          </StackPanel>

          <Border Classes="notice">
            <StackPanel Spacing="4">
              <TextBlock x:Name="ScriptedSessionPhaseState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="ScriptedSessionPhaseState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
              <TextBlock x:Name="ScriptedSessionProgressState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="ScriptedSessionProgressState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         Opacity="0.7"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
              <TextBlock x:Name="ScriptedSessionAbsenceState"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="ScriptedSessionAbsenceState"
                         Foreground="#FFE8E0EE"
                         Opacity="0.7"
                         FontSize="12"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
            </StackPanel>
          </Border>
        </StackPanel>
      </Panel>
      </DockPanel>
    </Border>
  </Grid>
</UserControl>
